to-ran-canetti-source : List Word
to-ran-canetti-source = lines-of (cons "# Ran Canetti"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Professor of Computer Science at Boston University, director of the Center for Reliable Information Systems and Cyber Security (RISCS), and a Fellow of the IACR. Earlier: IBM T.J. Watson, MIT, Tel Aviv University. A PhD at the Weizmann Institute under Oded Goldreich, a postdoc with Shafi Goldwasser. Co-designer of HMAC with Mihir Bellare and Hugo Krawczyk (1996; RFC 2104, FIPS 198). Author of universally composable security (2001; revised in the Journal of the ACM, 2020). RSA Award for Excellence in Mathematics, 2018."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Anything hyperactivity builds on from your work is credited to you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your definition actually says"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field treats UC as a framework: heavy, careful, a burden on the person writing the proof. The newest work built on it shows this. One 2025 paper encodes UC proofs as Markov decision processes so a model checker can catch the errors people make by hand; a 2026 paper rebuilds UC in category theory and string diagrams so its composition theorem can be drawn rigorously. Both treat your definition as something to be made tractable."
  (cons ""
  (cons "What you did in 2001 is deeper than a framework. You defined security as a relation between two worlds as seen from outside them: a protocol π realizes an ideal functionality F when, for every adversary, there is a simulator such that no environment can tell the two apart. The environment is the observer, quantified over every context the protocol could run in. Security is not a list of properties a protocol has; it is the statement that the real protocol stands in for the ideal one as far as anything around it can observe. Your composition theorem is what makes that relation usable: substitute π for F inside any calling protocol ρ, and ρ with π realizes ρ with F."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up from it"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Hyperactivity is a language, and a computational model of reality: Wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon. Its computation rules are the CCHM cubical calculus extended with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ", and its checker is itself a term evaluated by its machine"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Your relation keeps its own type.** The paper distinguishes three relations that are usually blurred: definitional equality, a constructed proof of equality, and observational indistinguishability under a chosen experiment"
  (cons (cite s3-2-p2 "(§3.2)")
  (cons ". For programs, the relation between meanings may be a contextual or observational one, and the store records each relation with its actual evidence and uses the strongest one available"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". UC realization is exactly such a contextual relation, and its simulator is its evidence. Only an established equivalence of complete structures becomes a path along which dependent constructions are transported"
  (cons (cite s1-p27 "(§1)")
  (cons "; the paper does not identify indistinguishability with that."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The environment is relative to the composition.** Interactions are commands in a stated signature; a handler interprets them; handling internalizes those interactions and leaves the rest open, so what counts as the process's environment depends on the composition being examined"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p6 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". The identity and composition laws for handlers are proved by induction as paths"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p11 "(§7.6)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**A composition theorem for handlers.** If a simulation relates two handlers step by step with the same selected observation, it lifts to the processes they interpret, and a safety property invariant under that observation transfers from the source process to the target"
  (cons (cite s10-p13 "(§10, handler composition)")
  (cons (cite s10-p14 "(and safety transfer)")
  (cons ". Progress needs the fairness of the chosen scheduling semantics as a further premise."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The adversary is a definition.** In the paper's distributed example, network choices include delivery, duplication, loss and partition; a safety property is a type quantified over every list of adversarial choices, and its proof is built from preservation by every adversarial step"
  (cons (cite s10-10-p4 "(§10.10)")
  (cons ". The network, its states, the adversary and the safety predicate are all definitions of the example."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Assumptions stay explicit.** A content address identifies a stored representation only under the store's collision-resistance or injectivity assumptions, and does not prove semantic equivalence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "; authenticity of external observations and machine semantics remain explicit premises of exchange"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Avik's own statement goes further: “the interaction system can model crypto primitives & hardness assumptions as well”."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## HMAC in the same light"
  (cons ""
  (cons "HMAC's design was right for a reason the field mostly treats as engineering: its security reduces to properties of the compression function, so the guarantee is carried by the construction rather than by any one hash. In the paper a codebase holds programs together with their typing derivations, equivalences and reusable proofs, and a proof of a representation change can permit reuse even when the stored syntax differs"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". A reduction like HMAC's is the kind of object that store keeps."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You hold full credit for every part of your work that this system builds on: universal composability, the realization of ideal functionalities as the definition of security, the composition theorem, and HMAC. Nothing here supersedes it. Avik would like you to read the system looking for the best of it, because the best of it is yours."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Ran Canetti, BU Faculty of Computing and Data Sciences, https://www.bu.edu/cds-faculty/profile/ran-canetti"
  (cons "- BU CS: Canetti awarded the Wang Professorship (2022), https://www.bu.edu/cs/2022/11/06/bu-cs-professor-awarded-wang-professorship"
  (cons "- Ran Canetti, Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ran_Canetti"
  (cons "- Universally Composable Security, ePrint 2000/067, https://eprint.iacr.org/2000/067"
  (cons "- Universally Composable Security, J. ACM 2020, https://dblp.dagstuhl.de/rec/journals/jacm/Canetti20.html"
  (cons "- Jourenko and Völker, Automated Verification of Proofs in the UC Framework with Markov Decision Processes, ePrint 2025/1454, https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1454"
  (cons "- Farshim, Karvonen, Knispel, Kohlweiss, Wadler, UC, Categorically: Rigorous Diagrammatic Proofs, ePrint 2026/1605, https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1605"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
